<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Ancient Gods</h1><p class="path-tagline">The gods of the ancient world, their temples, priests and myths, and how their worship ended.</p><p class="path-intro">For thousands of years the peoples of the Near East and Europe honoured many gods. Every Mesopotamian city had a patron god living in its temple, Egyptian kings upheld maat, the order of the world, Greek cities held festivals and games for the Olympians, and Romans exchanged prayer and sacrifice with the gods on the principle of “I give that you might give”. Much of what is known comes from tablets, inscriptions and texts written down later by Christians. This path follows these religions from the invention of writing in Mesopotamia to the last Slavic holy places of the 14th century.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inanna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 3500 BCE – c. 500 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mesopotamian religion</span><span class="path-step-why">Attested from the invention of writing around 3500 BCE, it gave every city a patron god, Marduk at Babylon and Ashur at Assur, served in a temple run as a household.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Epic of Gilgamesh</span><span class="path-step-why">The poem of the king of Uruk and his search for immortality, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, best known from Ashurbanipal’s library at Nineveh.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Epic of Gilgamesh is an ancient Mesopotamian poem about the legendary king of Uruk and his search for immortality.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 3100 BCE – c. 537 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ancient Egyptian religion</span><span class="path-step-why">For more than three thousand years Egypt worshipped Ra, Osiris, Isis and Amun to uphold maat, and its hope of eternal life inspired the pyramids and mummification.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/canaanite-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asherah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · c. 3000 BCE – c. 400 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Canaanite religion</span><span class="path-step-why">Its pantheon of El, Asherah and the storm god Baal is known chiefly from the tablets of Ugarit, and Phoenician settlers carried it west as the Punic religion.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paestum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 1400 BCE – c. 500 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ancient Greek religion</span><span class="path-step-why">With no sacred book and no single priesthood, the Greeks honoured Zeus and the Olympians through sacrifice, festivals, oracles and games such as the Olympics.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/celtic-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · c. 550 BCE – c. 600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Celtic religion</span><span class="path-step-why">Known from archaeology, often hostile Greek and Roman writers and medieval Irish and Welsh tales, it had druids, sacred groves and over two hundred named deities.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/roman-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · c. 509 BCE – 391 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Roman religion</span><span class="path-step-why">A religion of correct practice rather than belief, in which prayer, vow and sacrifice were exchanged with the gods on the principle of do ut des, “I give that you might give”.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · d. 395 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Theodosius I</span><span class="path-step-why">The emperor who made Nicene Christianity the official religion of the Roman state, and the last to rule both halves of the empire before its permanent division.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Theodosius I was a Roman emperor who died in 395 CE, the last to rule both the eastern and western halves of the empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/norse-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 500 CE – c. 1100 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Norse religion</span><span class="path-step-why">The religion of Odin, Thor, Freyr and Freyja, displaced by Christianity between the 10th and 12th centuries and known mainly from Icelandic texts of the 13th.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/slavic-paganism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svantevit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · c. 500 CE – c. 1331 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Slavic paganism</span><span class="path-step-why">The faith of the thunder god Perun and many-headed idols, whose rulers turned Christian between 863 and 988 while the old rites lived on in a “double faith”.</span></span></a></li></ol>
